    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing).\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the owner identity key and duplicate detection to match the specification.\n\n**New `Soundex` utility** (`util/Soundex.java`) \u2014 a pure-function implementation of the American Soundex algorithm (first letter + three digits, with vowel/`h`/`w` handling and same-code coalescing), mirroring the existing `TelephoneNormalizer`/`EmailNormalizer` utility style.\n\n**`OwnerIdentity.identityKey`** \u2014 now returns `SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName))`. The third component changed from the household id to the Soundex of the last name, and the whole joined string is now hashed via `Sha256.hex`, so the key is a 64-char lower-case hex digest (satisfying the acceptance test's shape check). The `Owner` overload feeds `lastName` instead of `householdId`.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1`:**\n- `rejectDuplicateIdentity` now compares the create's full `identityKey` against every active (non-deleted) owner's `identityKey` and throws `409` on an exact match \u2014 the single identity-key rule. The email-domain blocklist still runs first (unchanged, in `normalizeEmail`).\n- Removed the separate `rejectHouseholdDuplicate` call and method \u2014 the household-duplicate `409` block no longer applies.\n- `findPossibleDuplicate` (soft match) now triggers when the `identityKey` differs but `soundex(lastName)` **and** postcode match, returning the oldest such owner. Consequently, two owners sharing last name + postcode but with different telephones now have differing keys and are created as a soft match rather than rejected.\n\n**Removed** the now-dead `DuplicateOwnerHouseholdException` class and its import.\n\nI also updated the Javadoc across the touched classes so the documented behaviour matches the new single-identity-key model.",
